fma(x, y, z) with z zero gives the product's sign when the product
rounds to zero: musl's code (options/ansi/musl-generic-math) added z to
x*y rounded, so that fma(1e-300, -1e-300, 0.0) was +0.0, where the
exact result, a tiny negative number, rounds to -0.0, as glibc and the
FMA instruction give. x and y are finite and nonzero there, so x*y
rounded once is the result. CPython's math.fma checks it. Meant for
upstream, in musl too.

diff --git a/options/ansi/musl-generic-math/fma.c b/options/ansi/musl-generic-math/fma.c
index f65eab7..bf33a0c 100644
--- a/options/ansi/musl-generic-math/fma.c
+++ b/options/ansi/musl-generic-math/fma.c
@@ -64,7 +64,7 @@ double fma(double x, double y, double z)
 		return x*y + z;
 	if (nz.e >= ZEROINFNAN) {
 		if (nz.e > ZEROINFNAN) /* z==0 */
-			return x*y + z;
+			return x*y; /* x*y is not 0: its sign, even if it rounds to 0 */
 		return z;
 	}
 
